Synthesis of nano zinc-based metal–organic frameworks under ultrasound irradiation in comparison with solvent-assisted linker exchange: Increased storage of ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$
Amir Reza Abbasia,b,⁎, Ayda Moshtkoba, Nahid Shahabadic,d, Mohammad Yaser Masoomie, Ali Morsalif,⁎
a Faculty of Chemistry, Razi University, 67149 Kermanshah, Islamic Republic of Iran b Institute of Nano Science and Nano Technology, Razi University, Kermanshah 67149, Islamic Republic of Iran c Department of Inorganic Chemistry, Faculty of Chemistry, Razi University, Kermanshah, Islamic Republic of Iran d Medical Biology Research Center (MBRC), Kermanshah University of Medical Sciences, Kermanshah, Islamic Republic of Iran e Department of Chemistry, Faculty of Science, Arak University, Arak 38156-8-8349, Islamic Republic of Iran f Department of Chemistry, Faculty of Sciences, Tarbiat Modares University, P.O. Box 14155-4838, Tehran, Islamic Republic of Iran
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
Metal–organic framework   
$\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption capacity   
Solvent-assisted linker exchange   
Isostructural
Isostructural nano $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), $Z\mathrm{n}$ -TMU, has been synthesized by ultrasound process and solvent-assisted linker exchange (SALE). $\mathrm{Zn(II)}$ -MOFs were investigated as $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture compounds. Compared with ultrasound process, the as-prepared daughter frameworks showed enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption capacity, ascribed to the existence of structural defects during the SALE method. This study demonstrated that the gas storage depends mainly on the quality and defects in the structure, which depends on the synthetic conditions. This study is a new report of increasing $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption in $Z\mathbf{n}$ -TMU by a SALE method.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous coordination polymers (PCPs) that consist of metal ions coordinated to organic ligands resulting in a three-dimensional structure [1–3]. They have received considerable attention for several applications, including, but not limited to, separation and storage because of the large pore volume and surface area, the well-defined and accessible cages and metal sites and, finally, the ease by which the porosity, chemical properties and thermal conductivities can be adjusted [4,5]. Recently, the effectiveness of the SALE (i.e. solvent-assisted linker exchange) procedure was illustrated in the synthesis of various MOFs that was found difficult to obtain via de novo methods [6–10]. Briefly, SALE is a post-synthesis modification technique that includes changing the ligands in a virgin MOF with ligands of choice [6]. This technique is known to bridging-linker exchange that includes a heterogeneous reaction of as-prepared compound with a concentrated solution of ligands. In this reaction, daughter frameworks such as the primary structure are created [11]. The problem of insolubility or low solubility of the linkers has been solved by SALE. As a result, SALE occurred not only in the MOFs, but also in different solid-state systems. Morsali and co-workers [12,13] were the first to report on the TMU MOFs that have been studied as promising candidates for gas storage [12], uptake and separation material [14], removal and extraction of heavy-metal ions [15], drug storage [16], sensing [17], and catalysis [6,18,19].
In this paper, we report the synthesis and characterization of three porous $\mathrm{Zn(II)}$ -based TMU MOFs under SALE conditions in terms of TMU-4, TMU-5, and TMU-6, i.e. $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(o b a)_{2}(4\mathrm{-bpdb)]\cdot(DMF)_{2}}}$ , $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(o b a)_{2}(4-\ell)}$ bpdh)] $\cdot(\mathrm{DMF})_{3},$ and $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(o b a)_{2}(4\mathrm{-bpmb)]\cdot(DMF)_{3}}}$ , respectively, where oba, 4-bpdb, 4-bpdh, 4-bpmb, and DMF stand for $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -oxybisbenzoic acid, 1,4-bis(4-pyridyl)-2,3-diaza-1,3-butadiene (denoted L4), 2,5-bis(4-pyridyl)-3,4-diaza-2,4-hexadiene (denoted L5), and $\mathrm{\DeltaN^{1},N^{4}}$ -bis-((pyridin-4-yl) methylene)benzene-1,4-diamine (denoted L6) and $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide, respectively. We show that SALE offers the control of structure and the pore size of the TMU MOFs to obtain TMU-4 having one-dimensional pores with apertures of about $5.3\times9\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ TMU-5 having three-dimensional, interconnected, narrow pores with an aperture of about $4.4\times6.2\mathring\mathrm{A}$ [12], and TMU-6 having 3-fold interpenetrated structure, one-dimensional pore channels with an aperture of about $9.1\times8.9\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ The structure of TMU-6 is similar to that of TMU-4 but different from that of TMU-5. In TMU-6 and TMU-4, the metal ion centers are connected to three O-donor ligands and one $N$ -donor ligand, while $\mathrm{Zn_{2}}$ -centers in TMU-5 are coordinated via four $o$ -donor ligands and one $N\mathrm{.}$ -donor ligand to form a so-called paddlewheel unit in a 3D network [15]. Because of its stable structure, TMU-5 is porous toward $\Nu_{2}$ (measured at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar), while TMU-6 and TMU-4 are nonporous. The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of TMU-4, TMU-5, and TMU-6 is 4, 510, and $3\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $77\mathrm{K},$ respectively. The sorption properties of assynthesized TMU-SALE MOFs were studied by $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption measurements. Compared with TMU MOFs, the as-prepared TMU-SALE MOFs showed enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption capacity. This study is the first report of raising $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption in Zn-TMU under the SALE process. As a result, a simple technique was created herein for the synthesis of an existing $\mathrm{Zn(II)}$ based TMU MOF with higher porosity [8,20].
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and physical techniques
## 2.2. Synthesis of isostructural zinc-based metal–organic frameworks under ultrasound irradiation
In this work TMU-4 was prepared from the reaction between Zn $(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , oba, and L4 in a molar ratio of 0.60:1.0:1.0 mmol respectively [12,13], under ultrasound irradiation. Beforehand Zn $(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ligand had been separately dissolved in water $\ensuremath{\left(10\mathrm{mL}\right)}$ and ethanol $(10\mathrm{mL})$ , respectively. The $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ solution was added to the ligand solution under ultrasound power. At the end of the sonication process, the product was washed with fresh $^{N,N}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) $(3\times20\mathrm{mL})$ and dried in vacuo. For the characterization of the products, scanning electron microscopy and Xray powder diffraction were used. The product yield was $80.5\%$ . TMU5 and TMU-6 were obtained using the same reaction conditions and ratios as used for the isolation of TMU-4, but using L5 or L6 instead of L4 (Scheme 1).
2.3. SALE of TMU-4 into TMU-6 (denoted TMU-4-SALE-L6).
$120\mathrm{mg}\mathbf{L}6$ were dissolved in $14\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. Next, $180\mathrm{mg}$ of TMU-4 were dispersed in the resulting solution and placed in a $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for 5 days, upon which the L6 was completely exchanged by L4 linkers. The next daughter frameworks were obtained using the same procedure except that the reaction time or temperature varied.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Comparison of $C O_{2}$ storage in ultrasound process and SALE method
Gas storage and size of the cavities depends on the quality and defects in the structure, which depends on the synthetic conditions. As-synthesized parents TMU MOFs were synthesized under ultrasound (US) irradiation. In order to investigate the role of reaction conditions on the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ storage, blank reaction was performed under SALE method. For the sake of investigating the morphology of the parents
MOFs, the SEM images of as-prepared MOF were studied. Fig. 1 shows scanning electron microscope (SEM) of as-synthesized parents MOFs. TMU-6 and TMU-5 are nanoparticles, while TMU-4 is in the shape of a rod with a hexagonal cross section. The average particle size for TMU6 is around $35\mathrm{nm}$ while, the average particle size for TMU-5 is over $93\mathrm{nm}$ . Comparison between US process and SALE method shows that particle size of US process is smaller than particle size of SALE method. This finding is fully in line with other studies on ultrasoundassisted synthesis of nano-particles [4,21]. In comparison to the SALE method, US provide unusual reaction conditions that cannot be realized by SALE process. US produce its effect via cavitation bubble. “These bubbles are generated during the rarefaction cycle of the wave when the liquid structure is literally torn apart to form tiny void which collapse in the compression cycle” [22]. “Another effect of ultrasound on nucleation is shortening the induction time between the establishment of supersaturation and the onset of nucleation and crystallization” [23].
SALE includes replacing the ligands in an as-prepared MOF with ligands of choice [6]. First, SALE was carried out on TMU-4 with the aim of replacing L4 with L6 (denoted TMU-4-SALE-L6). To do the linear $N\mathrm{.}$ -donor ligand exchange, TMU-4 $\mathrm{\nabla_{180mg)}}$ ) was immersed in a DMF solution of L6 and placed in an oven at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [8]. After 5 days, the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR of representative MOFs showed that all of the L4 had been exchanged by L6. Furthermore, no signal from L4 and TMU-4 could be detected by PXRD and $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR [6,8], pointing to full linker exchange (Figs. 2 and 3a). Next, replacement of L6, starting from TMU-6, with L4 was studied (denoted TMU-6-SALE-L4). Results show that ligand replacement had taken place to form TMU-4 (Figs. 2 and 3b). L6 is bigger than L4 but also has a lower basicity [15]. Consequently, the reaction of TMU-4-SALE-L6 occurs at a lower temperature than that of
TMU-6-SALE-L4. This strategy is repeated for TMU-5 with the aim of replacing L5 with L6 (denoted TMU-5-SALE-L6). TMU-5 was immersed in a DMF solution of L6 and placed in an oven at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After 10 days, framework of TMU-6 was confirmed by PXRD. After the reaction, the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of TMU-5-SALE-L6 showed that $95\%$ of the L5 had been replaced by L6 (Fig. 3c). Fig. 2 shows PXRD patterns of as-prepared TMU-6-SALE-L5. Results show that the crystallinity of TMU-6 is protected and that the framework is unchanged by SALE. As a result, L5 was not replaced by other L6 to form TMU-5. Last, we immersed the TMU-5 in a DMF solution of L4 at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 days (TMU-5-SALE-L4). After the reaction, PXRD patterns of the as-synthesized powder indicated no change in crystallinity. Similar conditions were applied to TMU-4-SALE-L5. The results indicated L5 ligands were partly replaced by L4 to form TMU-4. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) is consistent with ligand replacement via crystalto-crystal conversion. As a result of the ligand replacement, the new TMU MOFs were obtained (Fig. 4).
Table 1 demonstrates that the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption capacities are increased for as-synthesized TMU-SALE MOFs, ascribed to the existence of structural defects during the SALE reaction [24]. Pore size distributions were measured using the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) method. Recall that TMU-4 and TMU-6 are nonporous compounds toward $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , while TMU-5 is porous [13]. For example, the data show that the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{N}}_{2}$ sorption of TMU-5-SALE-L4 is about 3 times greater than that of TMU-5 itself (Fig. 5). The sorption behavior of TMU-6-SALE-L4, TMU-5-SALEL6, and TMU-4-SALE-L6 toward $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ revealed that they can adsorb about $70\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(13.9\mathrm{\textrm{~\textperthousand}}$ , $53.3\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(10.5\mathrm{~\~}\%\mathrm{{wt}})$ , and $60.2\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ $(11.8\mathrm{~\96wt})$ , respectively, at 273 K and 1 bar (Fig. 6). Hence, the sorption capacity of MOFs can be enhanced using the SALE method. The average particle size for SALE method is higher than those over US process. As a result, ultrasound irradiation induces a decrease in the channel length and size of the compounds. In nano-structures the surface area is decreased so that the amount of adsorption is decreased a little. Results suggest that the storage of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ over US process is lower than those over SALE method. This study demonstrated that the gas storage depends mainly on the quality and defects in the structure, which depends on the synthetic conditions.
Table 2 summarizes the sorption characteristics of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at 1 bar. In SALE MOFs the structural defects increased so that the amount of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption is increased.
